From b5acc89a920642019e164945ae887a6f2f8b308a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matthew Dowell <mbd06b+github@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 10:15:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 14/16] fix: a closed reader never replaces a command's own
 failure

Re-review FIX FIRST item 2. `exit_status()` (brit-cli/src/main.rs, still
the one place the status is decided) checked the closed reader before the
command's result, so `rev-parse --verify missing` with a closed stderr and
`-v rev-parse HEAD missing` with a closed stdout ended with 141 instead of
their genuine 128.

- The command's own result is judged first. An error, or a Git-shaped
  non-zero exit, keeps its status whatever happened to the reader.
- 141 only for a read-only command that otherwise succeeded: `Ok`, a zero
  exit, or an error that is nothing but its write meeting the closed
  reader (a typed `ErrorKind::BrokenPipe` in the chain: io, gix or
  serde_json; never message text), and only when a watched write saw it.
- `READ_ONLY_COMMANDS` is matched on the exact subcommand path; each
  admitted leaf is listed (`diff`, `diff tree`, `diff file`, ...).
- The `closed_reader::seen()` flag is documented as never reset (one
  command per CLI process); the unit tests pass the booleans directly.

Tests: unit `a_closed_reader_never_replaces_the_commands_own_failure` and
`read_only_commands_are_classified_by_their_exact_path` (both fail on a
stub of the previous ordering and prefix match); journey
`a_closed_reader_never_replaces_a_genuine_failure` (fails on e534083f0's
binary with 141). Git itself, killed by SIGPIPE, reports 141 for those
cases; keeping the failure's status is a deliberate difference.

